-- Tradewinds's text in English: the template for translations.
-- Generated by `python tests/run_tests.py locale write`; don't edit by hand.
-- This file isn't loaded by the game (English is built in).
--
-- To translate: copy it to Locales/<code>.lua, put the code in the NewLocale
-- line, add the file to Tradewinds.toc after Locales\Locale.lua, and replace
-- each right-hand side. `python tests/run_tests.py locale` checks the result.
--  * Keep the placeholders (%s, %d, %.1f) in the same order; the code fills
--    them in that order (no %1$s).
--  * Keep colour codes (|cffrrggbb ... |r) around the matching words, and
--    line breaks (\n).
--  * Chat commands (/tradewinds ...) stay in English, also the words typed
--    after them (on, off, play, ...).
--  * Cards, characters and places from World of Warcraft: use the official
--    name in your language's client where there is one.
--  * Use one word for each keyword (Guard, Swift, ...) everywhere: card
--    text, the tutorial and the Guide.
--  * Plural pairs ("%d card pack" / "%d card packs"): the first is used for
--    1, the second for every other number. If your language needs more
--    forms, word both so they work for any number ("Card packs: %d").
--  * Card faces are small: a card's name about 24 letters at most, the
--    short rules text about 40 (half that in Chinese and Korean). Longer
--    text is printed smaller.
local _, ns = ...
local L = ns.NewLocale("enUS")
if not L then return end

